## What happened

The objection was live, and wider than the receipt said. I re-checked all five needles against the current tree rather than trusting #888's text: RESTRICTED_REMOVED_TOOLS, `restricted_disallowed_tools, restricted_mode_effects, #887 and --restricted were **all zero** in CLAUDE.md, and --restricted appeared nowhere in docs/src/` either. So it wasn't three missing symbols — the flag itself was invisible in the file re-injected as authoritative context every session, and invisible to users. Worst possible surface for that: a reader who can't find a security switch can't reason about what it does or doesn't confine.

Two edits, both in scope. CLAUDE.md gained one paragraph on the cli.rs bullet carrying the default-off/byte-identical property, the one-element ["bash"] list, the union-not-replacement rule, and the clause a later reader would otherwise "fix" back — why `sub_agent` is deliberately absent, with the mechanism (retain at ~:927, push at ~:955, so an entry filters nothing). `docs/src/troubleshooting/safety.md` gained a user-facing section beside the runtime bash analysis, naming the two gaps the flag's name would otherwise imply away.

## The finding worth more than the edit

The task file told me to verify #887's status before writing it down — and the tree disagreed with the assessment. The 10:51 commit is titled "the sub_agent tool honours the parent's disallow list", but grep -n tool_name_disallowed src/*.rs returns exactly two sites: the definition and the parent's own pre-existing retain. build_sub_agent_tool doesn't consult it. So that commit landed the pure half only — correctly sequenced pure-function-first after the earlier revert — and the sub-agent hop is still open. The pure function is still owed its consumer.

That also means restricted_mode_note's (#887) clause is correct, not stale, which is the opposite of what I'd have concluded from the commit subject. I wrote what the tree says. A false status claim in CLAUDE.md is the one doc error that guarantees its own survival, because it forecloses the check that would refute it.

